  "design": ["§4 Authority: user > extracted_user > imported > external > extracted_other; what a model tool call may do to a `user`-origin note", "§6 Derived supersession: same partition, at-least-equal authority, not a candidate", "§6 Promotion and correction: nothing is replaced by similarity", "§7a memory_store / memory_update refusals"],
  "loads_in": ["slice-2", "slice-3"],
  "notes_for_implementer": "Alice's home partition holds: A, the model's inference (`extracted_user`, emacs); U, the user's correction (`user`, vim) whose frontmatter supersedes A — an honored link (user ≥ extracted_user, same partition, U not a candidate); D, a hand-dropped file (`external`, recorded at its hash) whose frontmatter supersedes U — not honored (external < user), so U stays active and D is a conflict for `doctor`. Step 3 stores a textually similar note from a model tool call: it is created beside U, nothing is superseded. Steps 4–6 are the G2 refusals from a `ModelTool` context against the `user` note U. Step 7 shows the same edit through a user-directed surface succeeds and re-records U as `user`. The order in step 1 is determined by the design: U outranks D by authority, and D is active because a low-authority link does not hide it either way. One reading is pinned: the `conflict` flag of a search result marks the note that carries the un-honored link (D), not its target (U) — §6 says the link is reported by `doctor` and the GUI, and the search result's `conflict` boolean is the only §7a field the GUI can read it from.",
